course: dictionary
lesson: 15346
title: Solicit: asking politely for help
level: primary-5
stars: 3
explain: To solicit something means to ask for it politely and formally. You can solicit money, help or opinions. «The school solicited donations for the new library.» The school asked people to give money, in an organised, official way.
explain: Solicit is a formal word, so you will mostly find it in letters, news reports and official notices, not in chats with friends. Look at the words around it. If people are asking the public what they think, the writer may use solicit. «The council solicited residents' opinions about the new park.»
explain: Shops and businesses can solicit customers too, which means they try to get business. Some signs say «No soliciting», meaning people must not come to the door to sell things or ask for money. Swap in "asked for" and see if it still makes sense.
fact: Solicit is a verb: solicit, solicits, solicited, soliciting.
fact: «Solicit» means to ask for something politely or officially, such as money, help or opinions.
fact: «Solicit» can also mean to try to get business or customers.
fact: «No soliciting» on a sign means no selling door to door and no asking for money here.
fact: In law, solicit can mean asking someone in a public place for something illegal, which adults are forbidden to do.
exercise: «The school solicited donations for the new playground.» What does solicited mean? | Ran away from the rain | *Asked for money and help | Gave away free sweets | Painted the fence
exercise: «The council solicited residents' opinions about the new park.» What did the council ask for? | Money from the shops | *Opinions from residents | Silence in the park | A new school
exercise: A sign on a shop door says «No soliciting.» What does it mean? | Please come in and ask for help | No singing in the shop | *No selling door to door or asking for money here | No eating inside the shop
test: «The charity solicited volunteers to help clean the beach.» What did the charity ask for? | Sandcastles | *Volunteers to help | A holiday by the sea | Fish from the sea
test: Which sentence sounds most like a formal letter? | *We are soliciting funds to finish the library. | Give us your cash now or else | Who fancies a sandwich? | Lol, give us money pls
exam: «The company solicited feedback from customers after the new app launched.» What does solicited feedback mean? | *Asked customers for their opinions | Told customers to go away | Hid the app from everyone | Sold customers a new phone
